The final and fifth premise is an important one. It's a philosophical one, but one that I wanted to introduce at the beginning of the course

156
00:22:45,480 --> 00:22:53,620
so that you understand where I'm coming from, where this course is coming from. Because many people say, okay, so happiness is important.

157
00:22:54,700 --> 00:23:06,300
We seek it. We have declarations, national declarations, individual declarations of how important it is for us. But that doesn't mean that it is important.

158
00:23:06,580 --> 00:23:12,260
Or the is, the fact that it is important, doesn't mean that we ought to do it.

159
00:23:15,090 --> 00:23:21,230
My argument here is that not only is it important, it also ought to be important.

160
00:23:21,870 --> 00:23:23,970
So first about the is.

161
00:23:24,490 --> 00:23:35,430
Happiness, whether we like it or not, whether it's consciously, subconsciously, whether it's explicit or implicit, for most people, not all, but for most people, it is the highest end.

162
00:23:36,530 --> 00:23:40,510
And again, we have constitutions safeguarding our pursuit of happiness.

163
00:23:42,050 --> 00:23:47,030
We spend a lot of effort, a lot of time thinking about it for ourselves as well as for others.

164
00:23:47,850 --> 00:23:55,510
Aristotle, over 2,000 years ago, happiness is the meaning and purpose of life, the whole aim and end of human existence.

165
00:23:56,770 --> 00:24:03,410
William James, in 1890 or in the varieties of religious experiences, writes,

166
00:24:03,410 --> 00:24:11,310
